# config/axes/Kconfig.build — The BUILD_TYPE axis and the test machinery (ktest, canaries, chaos, census, boot bench)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build type (owner decision, wave 15)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

choice
    prompt "Build type"
    default BUILD_TYPE_RELEASE
    help
      What the image is for. The test-only machinery below depends on it,
      so a release image cannot carry it whatever else the .config says.
      The defconfig names it (the board does not decide it): release is the
      default, and every development defconfig says BUILD_TYPE_DEV.

      Development (the qemu*, edge, robot-edge, experimental and micro
      defconfigs, every QEMU kernel the Makefile builds from
      $AZOS_OUT/build/qemu-dev.config, and `make vf2 BUILD_TYPE=DEV`): secure boot
      and mandatory OTA signatures start off (unsigned boot); gate canaries armed from
      the command line (CANARY_RUNTIME), fault injection (CHAOS), the
      in-kernel tests (KTEST) and the real-time block-I/O check
      (RT_BLOCK_IO_CHECK) may be enabled, and the image may embed the TEST
      signing key (tools/keys/test_pub.bin, the `dev-key` feature every
      QEMU kernel uses).

      Release (the default; the embedded, fleet, robot-embedded,
      robot-fleet, vf2, k1 and rpi5 defconfigs): those four
      options are forced off (a `ktest` or `chaos` cargo feature on a
      release .config does not compile), the build refuses the TEST key
      (the `dev-key` fallback in crates/core/topology/build.rs; every
      `make` board target through tools/check_board_keys.py), and the
      image needs the fleet key in TOPOLOGY_PUBKEY_PATH. Secure boot
      (SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED) and signed OTA (OTA_SIG_MANDATORY) start on
      here and can still be turned off by hand; Certifiable forces them.

      Certifiable (never a default; not on the QEMU board, whose kernels
      embed the TEST key through the `qemu` board feature's `dev-key`, the
      one board fact left here until that policy leaves the board feature):
      a release build that also has secure boot and
      mandatory OTA signatures on and cannot turn them off
      (SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED, OTA_SIG_MANDATORY). The signing kept apart
      from the build host (`make release`) is a later step.

      No cost: it selects what may be compiled.

    config BUILD_TYPE_DEV
        bool "Development: test machinery and the TEST key allowed"

    config BUILD_TYPE_RELEASE
        bool "Release: test machinery forced off, TEST key refused"

    config BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE
        bool "Certifiable: release, with secure boot and signed OTA forced on"
        depends on !BOARD_QEMU

endchoice

config SWITCH_CENSUS
    # cargo-feature: switch-census
    bool "Census of the yield-and-switch path, phase by phase"
    default n
    help
      Measurement only (crates/core/sched/src/swcensus.rs). Times each
      phase of a yield that switches (trap and user mode, syscall
      dispatch, the pick, the re-queue, the context-saving gate, the
      accounting, context_switch, the return) and counts switches into
      each task slot; DOMAIN_TASKINFO prints the per-switch averages since
      its previous call as `[SW-CENSUS]` lines at log level debug when at
      least SWITCH_CENSUS_DUMP_MIN switches happened in between. The clock
      is rdcycle (riscv64) or cntvct_el0 (aarch64): under QEMU -icount
      both count guest instructions. Meaningful at one hart (one set of
      globals for all harts).

      Off (default): not compiled, zero instructions. On: about ten
      instructions per phase boundary (eight per switch) and 800 bytes of
      counters.
      Maps to the kernel cargo feature `switch-census`
      (tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py).

config SWITCH_CENSUS_DUMP_MIN
    int "Fewest switches that make a census dump worth printing"
    range 1 1000000000
    default 1000
    help
      SWITCH_CENSUS prints at a DOMAIN_TASKINFO only when at least this many
      switches happened since the previous one, so the windows of a
      benchmark print and the bookkeeping calls between them do not.
      No cost while SWITCH_CENSUS is off; read once per DOMAIN_TASKINFO when
      on. Raise it when a workload calls DOMAIN_TASKINFO often.

config FILE_CENSUS_EVERY
    int "Backend operations per file-census line"
    range 1 1000000000
    default 20
    help
      The `file-census` vsbench diagnostic (crates/fs/fs/src/census.rs,
      kernel cargo feature `file-census`, off in every build that ships)
      sums where a backend file's open, read, close and write go and
      prints one per-operation average line every this many backend
      opens, and another every this many backend writes. 20: the `disk`
      lanes' 40 iterations fill two lines each, so one at least is all
      lane. Same on every ISA and profile: it sets a print period, not a
      resource.
      No cost while `file-census` is off; on, one modulo per backend open
      and write and one console line per period. Raise it to average a
      long lane over more operations; lower it to see a short one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# In-kernel test registry (wave 15)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config KTEST
    # cargo-feature: ktest
    bool "In-kernel tests (ktest): run every registered test at boot, then power off"
    depends on BUILD_TYPE_DEV
    default n
    help
      Compiles every `ktest!` and `ktest_late!` test (crates/core/ktest) and
      the runner (kernel/src/ktest.rs), and prints TAP on the console
      (`1..N`, `ok i - name`, `not ok i - name # reason`, one plan for both
      phases), then a summary line, then powers the machine off.
      Early phase (`ktest!`): after boot init, before the secondary harts
      wake and before any task runs, on the boot hart, in name order.
      Late phase (`ktest_late!`): in the `ktest-late` kernel task once the
      scheduler runs on every hart, in name order; these tests create probe
      tasks and block. A panicking test is reported `not ok` by the panic
      handler and ends the run (the kernel does not unwind).

      Off (default in every mode, development included): no test, no entry
      in `azos_ktest`, no runner; zero bytes and zero instructions. It is
      not on in the QEMU development configs because the runner ends the
      boot: it is a test kernel, built for the gate's `ktest` rows. On: the
      tests' code and 32 bytes of `.rodata` per test, one more kernel task
      when a late test exists, and the boot stops after the run. Maps to
      the kernel cargo feature `ktest` (tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py).
      Development builds only (BUILD_TYPE): the `ktest` feature on a release
      .config does not compile.

config KTEST_SEMIHOSTING_EXIT
    bool "ktest runner leaves QEMU through semihosting SYS_EXIT (aarch64)"
    depends on ARCH_AARCH64 && BUILD_TYPE_DEV
    default y
    help
      aarch64 only. QEMU's `virt` machine has no power-off that carries a
      status (PSCI SYSTEM_OFF exits 0 after a failed run as after a clean
      one), so with this on the ktest runner (cargo features `ktest` and
      `qemu`) ends the run with semihosting SYS_EXIT: QEMU exits 1 after a
      failed run, 0 after a clean one, and the gate's ktest rows read that
      status next to the TAP summary (riscv64 has the sifive_test finisher,
      x86_64 isa-debug-exit).
      QEMU must run with `-semihosting-config enable=on,target=native`
      (the gate's `ktest_row` passes it): without it the call is an
      undefined instruction and the run ends in a trap instead of a
      power-off. Off: PSCI SYSTEM_OFF, status 0 either way, and only the TAP
      lines carry the verdict. Cost: one `hlt` at the end of a ktest run;
      nothing in a kernel built without `ktest`.

config X86_QEMU_DEBUG_EXIT
    bool "Power-off and reset leave QEMU through isa-debug-exit (x86_64)"
    depends on ARCH_X86_64 && BOARD_QEMU
    default y
    help
      x86_64 QEMU only. With this on, `Boot::shutdown` writes 0 and
      `Boot::reboot` 2 to QEMU's isa-debug-exit port (0xF4) before the
      machine's own sequence, so QEMU (`-device isa-debug-exit,iobase=0xf4,
      iosize=0x04`, as every gate row passes) exits with status 1 or 5: a
      status the gate can tell from a hang or a reset. The write is made
      only when CPUID says a hypervisor runs the kernel; without the
      device it goes nowhere and ACPI S5 or the reset ladder follows.

      Off: the FADT's sequences alone (ACPI S5; RESET_REG, 0xCF9, the
      8042, a triple fault), as on a PC; QEMU then exits 0 on S5 or on a
      reset under `-no-reboot`. Only a BOARD_QEMU build offers it, so a
      board build never writes port 0xF4 here. Cost: one CPUID and one
      port write on the power-off and reset paths.

config KTEST_LATE_PRIORITY
    int "Priority of the late ktest runner task"
    range 1 30
    default 30
    help
      The `ktest-late` task (KTEST on, a `ktest_late!` test linked) runs the
      late tests at this priority, pinned to CPU 0; lower is more urgent,
      16 is the default task priority and 31 the idle task's. It spends its
      time asleep while the probe tasks it starts run at their own
      priorities, so it sits below every task in the system by default and
      does not steal a hart from the scenario a test watches. Raise its
      urgency only if a test itself must run ahead of boot tasks.
      Read only when KTEST is on; no cost otherwise.

config KTEST_LATE_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "Longest a late ktest waits for its probe task (ms)"
    range 100 600000
    default 60000
    help
      A late test that starts a probe task waits for it, sleeping 10 ms at a
      time, at most this long, then reports `not ok` (the probe did not
      finish). The longest probe today needs
      a few seconds under QEMU; the default leaves room for a loaded host.
      Raise it for slow emulation (-icount, TCG on a busy host); lower it to
      fail a hung run sooner. Read only when KTEST is on; no cost otherwise.

config CANARY_RUNTIME
    bool "Runtime gate canaries: canary=<name>[,<name>] on the kernel command line"
    default y if LOG_LEVEL_DEBUG
    default n
    depends on BUILD_TYPE_DEV && !SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED
    help
      Lets a gate canary be armed for one boot from the kernel command
      line (the device tree's /chosen/bootargs; QEMU -append), so a canary
      row boots the same kernel as its base row instead of building one
      with a canary cargo feature. Each `canary!("name")` site in the
      kernel (kernel/src/canary_rt.rs lists the names) breaks its own
      property only when its name is armed; an unknown name in the source
      is a compile error, an unknown name on the command line is ignored
      with a warning.

      Never on a hot path: a site costs one load and one bit test each
      time it runs, so the syscall, IPC, context-switch and fault paths
      keep cargo-feature canaries (compiled out, zero instructions).

      On by default in the QEMU development configs (the ones at
      LOG_LEVEL_DEBUG, which the gate builds). Impossible in a release
      build (BUILD_TYPE), and with SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED (the kernel also
      refuses it under the `secure-boot-enforced` cargo feature): a
      command line must not weaken a signed image. Off: the parser, the
      names and every site compile out (`canary!` is a constant false).
      On: one device-tree walk at boot (KERNEL_CMDLINE_MAX bytes on the
      boot stack) and ceil(CANARY_RUNTIME_MAX / 64) * 8 bytes of .bss.

config CAP_REVOKE_CHAIN_CANARY
    # cargo-feature: revoke-chain-canary
    bool "Canary: skip the revoked-ancestor check on a capability's use"
    default n
    depends on BUILD_TYPE_DEV && !SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED
    help
      A capability derived from one that was revoked is refused on its next
      use, before the subtree sweep frees it (crates/core/ipc/src/cap.rs,
      revocation_in_flight). This canary skips that check, so the derived
      capability keeps working until the sweep runs: the host row in
      tests/host/cap-tests must go red with it. Never in a real build; a
      development kernel that turns it on has a revocation window.

config SCHED_WAKE_PREEMPT_CANARY
    # cargo-feature: wake-preempt-canary
    bool "Canary: a wake-up does not ask the waking CPU to reschedule for a better class"
    default n
    depends on BUILD_TYPE_DEV && !SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED
    help
      A task woken onto the waking CPU while that CPU runs a worse dispatch
      class (or a numerically worse RT level, or a later DL deadline) sets
      that CPU's need_resched, so the next preemption point takes the switch
      (crates/core/sched/src/scheduler/wake/preempt.rs). This canary never
      asks, so the wake waits for the next tick: the host row
      `a_preempting_wake_on_the_same_cpu_sets_need_resched` in
      tests/host/sched-wake-tests and the ktest
      `sched_wake_preempts_a_worse_class_on_the_same_cpu` must go red with
      it. Never in a real build; a development kernel that turns it on has
      a wake-up latency of up to one tick for cross-class wakes.

config CHAOS
    # cargo-feature: chaos
    bool "Fault injection (chaos=<point>:<rate> on the kernel command line)"
    default y if KTEST
    default n
    depends on BUILD_TYPE_DEV && !SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED
    help
      Compiles the fault-injection registry (crates/core/chaos) and its
      six named injection points: frame-alloc (the page allocator answers
      out of memory), heap-alloc (the kernel heap answers null; test scope
      only, the kernel's heap allocations are infallible), ipc-send (a
      pipe write is refused), timer-wake (the timer sweep wakes
      sleepers CHAOS_TIMER_DELAY_US late), spurious-irq (the tick rings an
      IPI with no cause at another CPU) and disk-io (a block read or write
      answers an I/O error). A point is armed from the kernel command line,
      `chaos=<point>:<rate>[,...]` (one failure in <rate> calls, applied
      once boot init is done) with `chaos_seed=<n>`, or from a ktest; the
      `chaos_*` ktests check that each fault is refused cleanly and leaks
      nothing. The introspection tree's `chaos/` holds each point's rate and
      counts.

      On by default only in a KTEST kernel. Impossible in a release build
      (BUILD_TYPE; the `chaos` feature on a release .config does not
      compile) and with SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED: a command line must not be
      able to fail a signed image's allocations. Off: every site compiles
      to the code it was before (`fire` is a constant false). On: one load
      and a branch per site call while its point is disarmed, about 300
      bytes of .bss. Maps to the kernel cargo feature `chaos`.

config CHAOS_SEED
    int "Fault injection: default seed"
    range 1 2147483647
    default 1
    help
      The seed of every injection point's generator when the command line
      gives no `chaos_seed=`. The same seed, arming and call order fail the
      same calls, so a failure found under one seed replays under it.
      No cost.

config CHAOS_TIMER_DELAY_US
    int "Fault injection: how late an injected timer-wake fault wakes (us)"
    range 100 1000000
    default 2000
    help
      When the timer-wake point fires, the sweep runs as if the clock were
      this far behind, so a sleeper due within the window is woken on a
      later sweep instead (late, never lost). Larger values stress
      deadline-sensitive code harder; with a tickless idle the next sweep
      can come as soon as the next timer interrupt. No cost while CHAOS is
      off; one subtraction per fired sweep when on.

config BOOT_BENCH_ITERS
    int "Boot [BENCH-RES] sweep: iterations per microbenchmark"
    range 1 100000
    default 100
    help
      A `qemu` build that keeps info lines (LOG_LEVEL >= INFO) runs the
      robot bench sweep (`azos_bench::run_all`, 49 `[BENCH-RES]` lines)
      once, shortly after boot, in the `bench-boot` task. Each
      microbenchmark runs this many iterations (best of a few runs). At
      100 the sweep is up to ~2 G instructions on riscv64 (about 17 s of
      virtual time at `-icount shift=3`); time scales linearly. Raise it for steadier
      numbers, lower it to shorten boot scenarios. No cost in a build
      without the `qemu` feature or below LOG_LEVEL INFO.

config BOOT_BENCH_PRIORITY
    int "Boot [BENCH-RES] sweep: task priority"
    range 1 30
    default 28
    help
      Priority of the one-shot `bench-boot` task (lower is more urgent;
      16 is the default task priority, 31 the idle task's). The sweep
      never blocks, so whatever it outranks on its CPU waits for all of
      it: run from the behavior task (priority 14) it stopped the control
      loop and starved every less urgent task on hart 2 for the whole
      sweep. The default sits below every boot task, so the sweep takes
      only time no other task wants (its numbers can include preemption).
      Raise its urgency only to measure on an otherwise loaded system.
      No cost in a build without the `qemu` feature.
